-- title:
--   A capped reciprocal-sine bound on a short odd interval
-- statement:
--   Let $0\le x\le y$ with $y-x\le2q$, $q\ge2$, $(a,q)=1$, $2\alpha=a/q+\beta$, and $|\beta|\le q^{-2}$. For $A,B\ge0$ and any real phase $\theta$, the sum over odd natural numbers $n\in(x,y]$ of the capped reciprocal-sine term at $\pi\alpha n+\theta$ is at most
--   $$2A+\frac{2}{\pi}Bq\log(4q).$$
--   At a sine zero the term is defined to be the cap $A$. The interval-length equality case is included.
-- source:
--   Terence Tao, Every odd number greater than 1 is the sum of at most five primes, https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.6656, Sections 5 and 6. Original Lean source: Theorems/Thm_TaoFivePrimes_vaughan_typeI_safe_odd_vinogradov.lean, lines 312-407.

